WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.270
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.200
DALE MARKOWITZ: Hey, I'm Dale.

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.430
This is "Making with ML."

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.055
And today I'm going to show you
how this adorable little fox is

00:00:05.055 --> 00:00:09.045
the future of video
game interactions.

00:00:09.045 --> 00:00:12.015
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:20.590
So imagine you're playing
this sick new game where

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.700
you decide to visit
your local shopkeeper,

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.617
but the shopkeeper doesn't
have what you need.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:29.598
What if instead of
selecting from a list,

00:00:29.598 --> 00:00:31.640
you could ask for what
you want in plain English,

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.230
while the game uses
machine learning to come up

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:34.230
with a fitting response?

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.220
Or what about making
objects, like cats and trees,

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:43.040
more interactive, action the
game developers didn't even

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:44.930
consider?

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:48.500
Can ML expand the ways players
interact with the game world?

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.300
Today, I sat down
with Anna Kipnis,

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.360
a senior creative
technologist with Stadia Games

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.110
and Entertainment, and
we talked about how

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.400
machine learning can make
game worlds more interactive.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.817
ANNA KIPNIS: In
my lifetime, we've

00:00:57.817 --> 00:01:00.860
seen games go from very low
fidelity, like maybe two

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.780
colors, like "Pong" or
something like that,

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.360
to extremely realistic 3D.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.090
So you know, the visual
development of games

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:12.500
has really exploded, but I think
that the interactivity is still

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.565
really lagging behind.

00:01:13.565 --> 00:01:16.790
Like, we're not that far from
where we started, really.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:18.650
And so what I'm
really interested in

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.997
is kind of closing that gap.

00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:22.580
DALE MARKOWITZ: Why
is it so difficult

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:24.380
today to build a
game that supports

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.232
all of these rich, varied,
and spontaneous interactions?

00:01:27.232 --> 00:01:28.690
ANNA KIPNIS: Right
now, the process

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:32.120
is that the developers
have to anticipate

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.010
what all the questions
that the player might

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.030
ask of their world.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:38.932
Like, are they going to ask
about this specific weapon

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:39.890
or something like that?

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.190
What should happen as a
response to that, right?

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:46.130
So, at the moment,
the developers

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.740
have to account for
both the cool answers

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:52.070
that they can have in the world,
but also every single question

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:53.600
that the player might ever have.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.940
What semantic ML
allows you to do

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:59.030
is that it allows you as a
developer to focus mainly

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:00.573
on the interesting answers.

00:02:00.573 --> 00:02:02.990
DALE MARKOWITZ: The technology
Anna is talking about here,

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:05.990
semantic ML, is a way of making
sense of words or sentences

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.012
with machine learning.

00:02:07.012 --> 00:02:08.720
Given a set of words,
you can use a model

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.628
to see which pairs
are the most similar.

00:02:10.628 --> 00:02:12.170
For example, an ML
model can tell you

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.720
that a jetpack is more
similar to a flying broomstick

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.300
than it is to a garbage
can or an apple.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.190
You can also use semantic
ML to help pick out

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:20.970
likely responses to queries.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.870
So if I say, "I would
like some coffee,"

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.280
Semantic ML can tell me,
"here's a cup" is a better

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.080
response than "I hate ur butt."

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.500
Anna uses semantic ML to
describe her game worlds

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.570
in plain English and then
have a model infer how

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:33.770
the world should behave.

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:35.090
Let's take a look.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.470
ANNA KIPNIS: I've
labeled objects

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:39.050
using English that may be
interesting for the fox

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:40.180
to interact with.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.080
And in pink, you see
the sorts of actions

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.120
that the fox can do
on these objects.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.020
It can predict, OK, of the
list that you just gave me,

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.150
if this is what the
player said or did,

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:52.100
it will be able to
rank them from best

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:55.850
to worst in terms of what
is the most likely thing

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:58.280
that somebody in
conversation might have done.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.290
So, first, let's
say hi to everyone.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:03.230
It waves.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:09.690
And then let's see if we
can have some coffee here.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:11.335
So it goes to pick up the cup.

00:03:11.335 --> 00:03:13.622
DALE MARKOWITZ: So cute.

00:03:13.622 --> 00:03:17.220
ANNA KIPNIS: Yeah, then
it brings it to us.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.940
And the coolest thing is I
didn't program the Fox how

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.070
to answer these questions
or even, you know,

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:23.960
what the heck coffee is.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.515
I just labeled that object
that kind of looks like a cup,

00:03:27.515 --> 00:03:29.810
and I put it like a small mug.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:31.670
That's just English words.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.395
And semantic ML kind
of did everything else.

00:03:34.395 --> 00:03:36.020
You can type sentences,
they don't even

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.990
have to be particularly
spot on, right?

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:41.725
Like, you can say, "sofa,"
and it will know it's couch.

00:03:41.725 --> 00:03:43.100
I actually have
the system rigged

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.070
up so that you can just do them
straight up in Google Sheets.

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:48.500
And then you can try these
really bizarre things,

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.020
like get really stoked.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:50.750
I get excited.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:52.310
Cool, make some [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:52.920
Oh, whoops.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.580
It conjured a painting
because I typed

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:57.590
"Monet" instead of "money."

00:03:57.590 --> 00:03:59.480
And you know, this
thing actually

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:02.040
has cultural associations.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.950
And so it understands
that Monet is a painter,

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:08.890
and so it conjured a painting.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:09.522
So, yeah.

00:04:09.522 --> 00:04:11.355
And so here now that
it's spelled correctly,

00:04:11.355 --> 00:04:12.593
it creates a coin.

00:04:12.593 --> 00:04:14.260
DALE MARKOWITZ: What
you just showed me,

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.760
it's exactly what you're
saying where like, you

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:19.010
as-- even as the person
that built the demo,

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.345
it's still surprising
and spontaneous to you.

00:04:21.345 --> 00:04:22.220
ANNA KIPNIS: Exactly.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.910
This is like what's
so magical to me

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:27.110
is that there is
essentially a way

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:30.560
to condense some
of these patterns

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.780
that people have
in conversation,

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:36.470
condense all of that human
interaction into a usable form

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.600
that you can have in a game.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:40.520
To me, that's like,
it's essentially

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:42.350
indistinguishable from magic.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:43.680
DALE MARKOWITZ: If you want
to learn more about the tools

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.740
that Anna used to build her
demo and how you can use them,

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:48.170
make sure you check out
the blog in the link below.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.128
And let us know what you
build in the comments.

00:04:50.128 --> 00:04:51.670
See you next time.

